  "text": "To find the sum of the three positive real numbers \\( k \\) such that the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)(x - k)}{x} \\) achieves its minimum value at exactly two positive real numbers \\( x \\), we need to analyze the function and its critical points.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the function in a more convenient form:\n\\[ f(x) = \\frac{(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)(x - k)}{x} = (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)\\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x}\\right). \\]\n\nThe function \\( f(x) \\) will have critical points where the derivative \\( f'(x) \\) is zero or undefined. The derivative of \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\\[ f'(x) = \\left((x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)\\right)' \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x}\\right) + (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\left(-\\frac{k}{x^2}\\right). \\]\n\nSimplifying the derivative, we get:\n\\[ f'(x) = (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x} - \\frac{k}{x^2}\\right) - \\frac{k(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)}{x^2}. \\]\n\\[ f'(x) = (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x} - \\frac{k}{x^2}\\right) - \\frac{k(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)}{x^2}. \\]\n\\[ f'(x) = (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x} - \\frac{k}{x^2}\\right) - \\frac{k(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)}{x^2}. \\]\n\\[ f'(x) = (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x} - \\frac{k}{x^2}\\right) - \\frac{k(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)}{x^2}. \\]\n\\[ f'(x) = (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\left(1 - \\frac{k}{x} - \\frac{k}{x^2}\\right) - \\frac{k(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)}{x^2}. \\]\n\nFor the function to achieve its minimum value at exactly two positive real numbers \\( x \\), the critical points must be such that the function has a local minimum at two points and a local maximum at another point. This implies that the function must have a double root at one of the points where the derivative is zero.\n\nThe function \\( f(x) \\) will have a double root at \\( x = 18, 72, 98 \\) if \\( k \\) is such that the function \\( (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\) has a double root. This means that \\( k \\) must be the value that makes the function \\( (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\) have a double root.\n\nThe sum of the roots of the polynomial \\( (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) \\) is \\( 18 + 72 + 98 = 188 \\). Since \\( k \\) is the value that makes the function have a double root, the sum of the three values of \\( k \\) must be such that the function has a double root at one of the points.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the three values of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{256}. \\]",
